==Strategy==
*Only use killing roles if you really need someone dead. If you have no killers, a {{A|Tavern Keeper}} or a {{A|Bootlegger}} is a slightly less-effective alternative to keep them occupied for the {{A|Night}}. Hopefully, it will buy you time to get a more permanent solution.
*If you think that someone in the {{A|Coven}} or an ally is going to be attacked, {{A|Reanimate}} a {{A|Town Protective}} role and protect them.
**You can use this to protect a {{A|Stone Gazing}} {{A|Medusa}} with no risk to yourself unlike the {{A|Potion Master}}.
**Alternatively, if you know who the attacker is, consider reanimating a {{A|Tavern Keeper}} or {{A|Bootlegger}} to [[roleblock]] them. This will buy your friend an extra {{A|Night}} to prepare.
*Keep other powerful roles with the abilities to kill, protect, or heal in the late game. This will serve to sustain your faction, especially when you are the only member left.
*Later in the game, you may select yourself to summon {{A|Ghouls}}, or you may continue to use dead corpses. Planning with the other {{A|Coven}} members on when to use a corpse and when to attack will help in providing the most efficient use of your role.
*{{A|Crusaders}} can also be your toys to kill enemies. Using their ability to visit important members such as the {{A|Mayor}} or {{A|Psychic}} can help you kill members trying to protect or watch over them.
*Several roles can be used to confuse the {{A|Town}} about the role list or to fake a claim yourself:
**Controlling the {{A|Vigilante}} can be used for deception. Accidentally killing an evil role makes you able to claim {{A|Vigilante}}, since the message will say that the {{A|Vigilante}} killed them.
***This should be done with caution, however, since smart {{A|Townies}} will often suspect a {{A|Necromancer}}, especially if you've done anything else that makes your presence obvious.
**Other killing roles, like {{A|Crusaders}}, can be also used for tricking the {{A|Town}} to make them look like you did the job.
***However, make sure your claim fits on the role list. Otherwise, you'll be spotted as a {{A|Necromancer}} and hanged almost instantly.
***Additionally, {{A|Lookouts}} that watch your target will see your zombie, and {{A|Trackers}} that watch you will see you visit a dead person; either of these will make your deception obvious.
***Note that you can fake roles like this for other members of the {{A|Coven}}, not just yourself.
**Controlling a {{A|Guardian Angel}} will produce an immediate message to the entire {{A|Town}}, which can be useful for claiming those roles yourself, though {{A|Town}} members may grow suspicious at the coincidence of a second {{A|Guardian Angel}} claiming to protect the same target as the first.
*All actions against someone jailed by the {{A|Jailor}} will fail, including those you control as a corpse. If a {{A|Coven}} member is imprisoned, it is not advised to use a corpse against them. Instead, focus on finding or potentially killing the {{A|Jailor}}.
*Your role is not very useful early-game, when not many roles are dead, let alone killing or protecting roles. However, later in the game, the diversity the {{A|Necromancer}} is capable of swinging the tide in the {{A|Coven's}} favor.
*Since you don't visit the target yourself, you can attack a {{A|Veteran}} or someone who is guarded by a {{A|Town Protective}} role at little risk to yourself. In fact, sending a zombie to attack someone who is guarded by a {{A|Bodyguard}} is an excellent way to kill said {{A|Bodyguard}}, removing a significant threat to the {{A|Coven}}.
*You can use a dead {{A|Bodyguard}} (or any other {{A|Town Protective}}) to protect a fellow {{A|Coven}} member. Note, however, that if a {{A|Lookout}} is watching that member, it will cast suspicion on the {{A|Coven}} member if you die and are revealed to be a {{A|Necromancer}}. Why would the {{A|Necromancer}} use a dead {{A|Bodyguard}} to protect someone if they aren't a part of the {{A|Coven}}? Therefore, only use this as a last-ditch effort to save their life if you know or suspect that a {{A|Lookout}} exists.
*You can use the zombie of a {{A|Tavern Keeper}} or {{A|Bootlegger}} to [[roleblock]] a {{A|Serial Killer}} or {{A|Werewolf}} safely. Be careful when blocking a {{A|Werewolf}}. Make sure no other {{A|Coven}} members are visiting them, since you're forcing the {{A|Werewolf}} to stay home and kill all visitors.
*If you have the {{A|Necronomicon}} and there are dead killing roles whose corpses you haven't used yet, you may want to use them to attack instead of your ghoul. This will delay revealing to the {{A|Town}} that you have the {{A|Necronomicon}}, leaving more ambiguity as to the composition of the {{A|Coven}}. However, if you have roles that have a {{A|Powerful Attack}} (like the {{A|Werewolf}}), it is generally recommended to save them for more immune roles like the {{A|Godfather}}.
*You can use a deceased {{A|Arsonist}} to douse someone, permanently changing their investigative results. This is occasionally useful on {{A|Town}} to make them look like the {{A|Godfather}}, or on fellow {{A|Coven}} when their results would otherwise be even more suspicious; when dousing {{A|Coven}}, however, be wary of the possibility of a second {{A|Arsonist}}!
**If you suspect a second {{A|Arsonist}}, save the {{A|Arsonist}} corpse for when you can find them. When you do, you can either {{A|Douse}} them to force them to waste a {{A|Night}} cleaning themselves. or time your {{A|douse}} to kill the {{A|Arsonist}} when they ignite.
**You can alternatively use a {{A|Framer}} corpse to frame fellow {{A|Coven}} when their results would otherwise be even more suspicious without the risk of a second {{A|Arsonist}}.
*{{A|Trackers}} are quite dangerous to you; if they visit you on a {{A|Night}} when you reanimate someone, they will see you visit the [[graveyard]]. Keep in mind that the {{A|Retributionist}} is the only other role that visits the [[graveyard]]; your only options are therefore to either claim {{A|Retributionist}} (provided you reanimated a {{A|Townie}}) or to claim you were transported when a {{A|Tracker}} calls you out. Alternatively, you could try to cast doubt on the {{A|Tracker}} directly.
**If you suspect a {{A|Tracker}} will visit you on a given {{A|Night}}, you should either stay home or go with a {{A|Retributionist}} claim and reanimate a {{A|Townie}}. Alternatively, you could use a dead {{A|Crusader}} or {{A|Ambusher}} to kill them if one is available.
*The {{A|Forger}} can be a dangerous threat; they can deny you corpses to use if they forge someone as a non-visiting role, make your {{A|Retributionist}} claim look suspicious if you claim it, or even trick you into accidentally killing fellow members of the {{A|Coven}} by forging a killing role as a {{A|Town Protective}} role like {{A|Doctor}} or {{A|Bodyguard}}.
**If you suspect a {{A|Forger}} has forged someone in the [[graveyard]], don't use the corpse to protect your {{A|Coven}} members as they could be a killing role. {{A|Forgers}} should be dealt with as soon as possible before they forge a role that could have saved you the game.
*You can use your ability to manufacture {{A|Mafia}} visits for the {{A|Spy}}. If there's a revealed spy announcing results, it can sometimes be useful to send harmless {{A|Mafia}} like a {{A|Framer}} to visit a fellow Coven member in order to make it clear they're not Mafia.
*If you send a {{A|Vampire}} to someone who can be converted, you risk creating an additional enemy; for the most part, {{A|Vampires}} are more dangerous to you than {{A|Town}} is since they kill you when they try to convert you. Deliberately turning someone into a {{A|Vampire}} is therefore rarely a good strategy.
**If you are ''certain'' someone is {{A|Mafia}}, you can use a {{A|Vampire}} to kill them; doing so has the added advantage of making both the {{A|Town}} and the {{A|Mafia}} believe that there are still {{A|Vampires}} left, which may draw their attention from you and get otherwise-confirmed people hanged.
**If you are ''certain'' that someone is protected by a {{A|Bodyguard}}, sending a {{A|Vampire}} at them can kill the {{A|Bodyguard}} without wasting a killer's corpse.
**Since using a {{A|Vampire}} to deliberately convert someone is such an unusual strategy, you can occasionally use it for complicated tricks. For instance, you can claim {{A|Lookout}} or {{A|Tracker}}, send a dead vampire convert someone, and say you saw the visit, then either convince the {{A|Town}} to hang the new {{A|Vampire}} or kill them yourself at {{A|Night}} in order to get the person you accused of visiting them hanged.
**If you absolutely must remove someone's ability without killing them, sending a {{A|Vampire}} to visit them is an option.
**Sending a vampire to bite the {{A|Mayor}} is usually a good strategy. If they're guarded by a {{A|Bodyguard}}, the {{A|Bodyguard}} will die; if not, you can instantly expose them as a {{A|Vampire}} the next morning at any time by whispering to them before they have a chance to do anything.
***Just make sure you're not accidentally giving {{A|Vampires}} a majority by doing this.
***Also be aware that if the other {{A|Vampires}} are dead and the {{A|Mayor}}-turned-{{A|Vampire}} may see they're the last {{A|Vampire}}, they can reasonably deduce that their accuser is likely to be a {{A|Necromancer}} and may be able to convince the {{A|Town}} of this even if they get hanged; meanwhile, if other {{A|Vampires}} ''are'' alive, they may realize that the extra, unexpected conversion must have come from a {{A|Necromancer}} and therefore guess that that {{A|Necromancer}} is probably the former {{A|Mayor's}} main accuser.
*You can reanimate a dead {{A|Pestilence}}, but you will be attacked by them for visiting them even in the [[graveyard]]. Therefore, this should only be used as a last resort or if you have a {{A|Potion Master}} heal you.
*You are one of the very few roles that can deal with {{A|Pestilence}} safely, provided the right corpses are available:
**If a {{A|Bodyguard}}, {{A|Crusader}}, or {{A|Doctor}} is available, you can use it to protect someone from {{A|Pestilence}} for one {{A|Night}}. When using a {{A|Bodyguard}} or {{A|Crusader}}, {{A|Pestilence}} themselves will, of course, not die, but the zombie will still save whoever you assigned them to.
**When {{A|Pestilence}} is present, you may want to avoid killing in order to keep the votes you'll need to hang them. If you're confident you'll still have the votes, though, you're also one of the few roles that can keep killing safely while {{A|Pestilence}} is around, since your indirect attack means you're immune to both their rampage and death from visiting them.
**Note. However, unlike the {{A|Retributionist}}, you can not use corpses on yourself. Meaning you can not directly save yourself from {{A|Pestilence}}.

==Dealing With {{A|Necromancers}}==
The {{A|Necromancer}} is a large threat to the {{A|Town}}, as whenever you think you've gotten rid of a threat the {{A|Necromancer}} can resurrect them for one {{A|Night}}.
*{{A|Trackers}} are a great help, as they can catch a {{A|Necromancer}} red-handed with ease. As a {{A|Tracker}}, if you see someone visit the [[graveyard]] (ie: Ann Sears is dead, you track John Willard to Ann Sears) call them out immediately. If they visit an evil role in the [[graveyard]], it's even better to call them out as there is no threat of them being a {{A|Retributionist}}.
*{{A|Lookouts}} can be useful in detecting the {{A|Necromancer}} if they see a dead person visit their target. If a dead player visits somebody, then a {{A|Necromancer}} or {{A|Retributionist}} is confirmed to be in-game. If said dead player is a {{A|Town Protective}} and visits someone who did not ask to be protected, then the person you are watching may be a member of the {{A|Coven}}.
*If, for instance, multiple {{A|Mafia}} roles kill in one {{A|Night}}, as in two kills by members of the {{A|Mafia}}, or multiple {{A|Neutral Killing}} deaths when only one can be available appear, a {{A|Necromancer}} is confirmed. {{A|Retributionist}} and {{A|Medium}} claims should be watched.
*If a {{A|Vigilante}} is asked to shoot someone suspected of being a member of the {{A|Coven}} and they are protected, with a dead {{A|Doctor}} or {{A|Crusader}}, anybody who claims to have protected them could be the {{A|Necromancer}}.
*If an {{A|Ambusher}} attacks a player, but one is in the [[graveyard]] already or all {{A|Mafia}} are dead, a {{A|Necromancer}} is still in the game.
*If you are a {{A|Medium}}, and a dead {{A|Guardian Angel}} tells you they haven't used their ability, there is a {{A|Necromancer}} still in the game.

