==Strategy==
The {{A|Witch}} is a potentially devastating role that has the ability to control someone's action. This can cause events such as a making a {{A|Serial Killer}} and {{A|Godfather}} kill someone that will make them more obvious, or causing a {{A|Doctor}} to heal you.

===Messing with {{A|Town}} roles===
*If someone reveals they are a {{A|Veteran}}, it's a good opportunity for you to control someone you want dead and make them target the {{A|Veteran}}; if the {{A|Veteran}} goes on {{A|Alert}}, whoever you controlled will die. If your first target is a killing role, however, they may end up killing the {{A|Veteran}} instead if they did not {{A|Alert}}.

*You can avoid a {{A|Sheriff}}, {{A|Investigator}}, or a {{A|Lookout}} from investigating you by having them investigating another role or themselves. Since you get the results they would have normally received, you may even use them to find an evil role.

*If an {{A|Investigator}} calls you out you are forced into two positions, claim you were being transported, or that you are a {{A|Lookout}}. The {{A|Investigator}} is one of your biggest foes, so making sure that {{A|Investigators}} aren't targeting you is one of your highest priorities. 

*Take advantage of {{A|Vigilantes}}. If one is revealed, forcing him to kill another {{A|Townie}}, such as a revealed {{A|Mayor}}, will be sure to anger the {{A|Town}} and may help you win. Make sure that they are not a {{A|Veteran}} though, as claiming {{A|Vigilante}} is a common strategy. However, the fact that there's a {{A|Witch}} in game might make it harder to control them. (i.e {{A|Jailor}} on, {{A|Tavern Keeper}} [[Role block|role blocking]] them or {{A|Transporter}} {{A|Transporting}} them)

*If you know who the {{A|Jailor}} is {{A|Jailing}}, you can force them to execute their target by controlling the {{A|Jailor}} to target the player in {{A|Jail}}. This can be devastating if the {{A|Jailed}} target is an important {{A|Town}} role, particularly the {{A|Mayor}}.
**Remember though, that it could be very hard to actually manage this. 
**This also works the other way around; if you do ''not'' want a {{A|Jailor}} to execute someone, such as a member of the {{A|Mafia}}, you can force them to target someone else, and they will not be executed.

*Control a known {{A|Tavern Keeper}} to [[role block]] dangerous {{A|Town}} roles, particularly {{A|Town Investigative}} or {{A|Town Killing}} roles.
**Even better, if you send a {{A|Tavern Keeper}} to a {{A|Werewolf}} (On {{A|Full Moon}} {{A|Nights}}), they will die and whoever they wrote in their {{A|Last Will}} as their target will come under suspicion (rather than the actual {{A|Werewolf}}); this can be particularly advantageous if you want to keep a {{A|Werewolf}} from targeting you or another evil buddy. You will also get rid of a {{A|Town}} that could dangerously [[role block]] {{A|Mafia Killing}}. Be cautious and to not do this to a {{A|Bootlegger}} (unless you're certain you want to side with other evils.), since you will kill an ally. Note that you will survive throwing a {{A|Tavern Keeper}} to a {{A|Werewolf}} in this manner.
**Furthermore, if a {{A|Mafia Killing}} role is being repeatedly [[Role block|role blocked]] by a {{A|Tavern Keeper}} and is unable to kill, you can liberate them by forcing the {{A|Tavern Keeper}} to target someone else, especially into a known {{A|Serial Killer}} or {{A|Werewolf}} on a {{A|Full Moon}}.
*Using your powers to kill {{A|Townies}} is not the only strategy a {{A|Witch}} can use! If you discover someone is a {{A|Doctor}} or {{A|Bodyguard}}, have them target you. You guarantee yourself protection every {{A|night}} until your unwilling victim is put out of commission. An added advantage of controlling a {{A|Bodyguard}} to protect yourself is that if the {{A|Mafia}} tries to kill you and hits your {{A|Bodyguard}} instead, and the {{A|Bodyguard}} indicated (in their {{A|Last Will}} or in chat) that they were being controlled, then the {{A|Mafia}} will learn that you're a {{A|Witch}} and will avoid attacking you in the future.
**If they are protecting someone important, like the {{A|Mayor}} or {{A|Jailor}}, this makes this strategy even better as this gives an opening for your {{A|Mafia}} friends to attack them!
**If the {{A|Mafia}} has an {{A|Ambusher}}, you can even ask the {{A|Ambusher}} to target you while you control {{A|Town Protectives}} into yourself, allowing the {{A|Mafia}} to reliably kill 2 {{A|Townies}} per {{A|Night}}! Alternatively you can control the {{A|Ambusher}} into other {{A|Mafia}} members to kill pesky {{A|Town Investigatives}} or a {{A|Tavern Keeper}}.

===Controlling evil roles===
*Controlling {{A|Mafia}} (especially the {{A|Godfather}}) may be dangerous as you can make them kill {{A|Mafia}}. To avoid this, next time you control a player whom you are unsure if they are {{A|Mafia Killing}} or not, make them target themselves. If they are the {{A|Godfather}}, the {{A|Mafioso}} will attack the {{A|Godfather}}, but not kill due to the {{A|Godfather's}} {{A|Basic Defense}}. However, this will make {{A|Mafia}} waste a kill, and a {{A|Spy}} may accuse the {{A|Godfather}} of having defense and push on them. If they are a {{A|Mafioso}}, they will most likely ignore your controlling and kill the {{A|Godfather's}} intended target. However, this is risky because the {{A|Godfather}} might be [[Role block|roleblocked]]/{{A|Jailed}}, not pick a target or simply not exist in-game. If they are an {{A|Ambusher}}, they will attack '''you'''. You will either die, or lose your {{A|Basic Defense}} from their attack, which can be detrimental later on.
*If you find a {{A|Neutral Killing}} role, you should decide between controlling them continuously or focusing your attention elsewhere. Controlling them continuously has the advantage of ensuring your safety, but it has the disadvantage of mostly wasting your capabilities otherwise (since - unless you use knowledge from previous controls to choose key targets for them - you're not actually helping them win).
**It's worth noting that continuously controlling a {{A|Neutral Killing}} role will usually annoy the target and can sometimes lead them to turning against you.
**Controlling a {{A|Werewolf}} is dangerous since if they're [[Role block|role blocked]], they'll stay home and kill all their visitors, including you. However, you can use them to deal powerful Rampage attacks to whoever you choose.
**Controlling an {{A|Arsonist}} into themselves will cause them to {{A|douse}} themselves. If you plan to team with a different evil role, this could be a plausible idea to hold them off. Note that if you don't control them for a {{A|night}} they may clean the gasoline off themselves.
***However, if you do {{A|douse}} an {{A|Arsonist}}, be VERY CAREFUL, as you are now {{A|doused}}.

===Revealing yourself to evil roles and teaming up with them===

*If you find a {{A|Neutral Killing}} role or who the {{A|Mafia}} are, or it would be best to team up with them because you can win with any non-{{A|Town}} roles. Sometimes it would be best to make friends with both to avoid being killed at {{A|night}}, but betraying one [[Factions (ToS)|faction]] may cause them to try to kill you. Your first target's role will be told to you.  When you find a non-{{A|Town}}, you can find a quiet way of talking to them, such as a [[Chat (ToS)#Whispering|whisper]] to target A, "I know what you are; we can work together."  But be careful, [[Chat (ToS)#Whispering|whispers]] in a {{A|Witch}} game are often viewed as {{A|Witch}} communicating to team up.  If you team up with a non-{{A|Mafia}} evil (unless you're deliberately whispering your real role to a {{A|Neutral Evil}} role for a {{A|Blackmailer}} to see), it may be risky to whisper them as a {{A|Blackmailer}} may be in game, hence giving you and the evil you side with away to the {{A|Mafia}}.
**It's generally not to your advantage to kill off the {{A|Mafia}}, since you can win with them; however, remember that unlike actual members of the {{A|Mafia}}, you always lose if you die and are capable of winning with the other evils. Therefore, you ''can'' kill a member of the {{A|Mafia}} if you intend to side with a {{A|Neutral Killing}} role and if the {{A|Mafia}} somehow becomes a threat to you. This isn't [[gamethrowing]], provided that you legitimately believe killing them increases your chances of surviving. Similarly, since you have no victory condition after you die, it isn't [[gamethrowing]] to name them in your {{A|Last Will}} (doing so can even be a useful threat to keep them from killing you). Part of playing the {{A|Witch}} is about building trust and cooperation with other evil roles, and having that trust fall apart is a legitimate part of the game. That said, siding with the {{A|Mafia}} is usually your best path to victory, so killing them should be avoided whenever possible.
***Another strategy is to [[Chat (ToS)#Whispering|whisper]] to the player you controlled that you visited them last {{A|night}} instead of outright claiming {{A|Witch}}.  As your visits leave a notification in chat, they should realize you are the {{A|Witch}}, and the {{A|Blackmailer}} may not suspect anything if you are communicating to a non-{{A|Mafia}}

*One way to identify yourself to an evil role the {{A|night}} after controlling them is to announce ''you'' were controlled the next morning. Assuming there's only been signs of one {{A|Witch}} up until now, they will ideally realize you're lying, which will hint to them that you're the actual {{A|Witch}}.
**Be careful, though. Some evil roles might not get the hint, and will still call you out. Or even miss the fact that they were controlled or just not relay that to the rest of the team and as a result make their team think you are {{A|Town}}.
**It is also not a good idea to identify yourself to a {{A|Neutral Killing}} like this unless they are in a better position than {{A|Mafia}}. Doing this twice in a single game will give both sides a clear message that you're two-timing and make both of them wary of you. Not that it's a secret that you are, but nonetheless it may irritate them.
*You could try and remain silent the whole game and not control anyone. This makes it so {{A|Lookouts}} can't see you and players may think the {{A|Witch}} is away from keyboard, prompting them to target silent players..
**Keep in mind that many evil roles tend to kill quiet players because they are often important {{A|Town}} members. Alternatively, the {{A|Town}} often targets quiet players because many evil roles tend to stay quiet as well.
**The downside is that an {{A|Investigator}} can easily bust you, especially in {{A|Ranked Practice}} or {{A|Ranked}}, and if you claim to be a different role you may be executed or [[hanged]] otherwise.
*It is generally a better idea overall to team up with the {{A|Mafia}} or {{A|Vampires}} rather than the {{A|Serial Killer}} if you have the choice. If it comes down to you, the {{A|Serial Killer}}, and the {{A|Godfather}}, the {{A|Serial Killer}} will likely kill you in order to secure their victory, whereas the {{A|Godfather}} needs you alive in order to [[hang]] the {{A|Serial Killer}}.
*While you can win with the {{A|Werewolf}} as well, you're often better off siding with someone else and trying to have them [[hanged]]. This is because the {{A|Werewolf}} is both [[unique]] and a visiting role. You're at risk of being killed by the {{A|Werewolf}} even if they know you're on their side.
**You can coordinate kills with the {{A|Werewolf}} such that you can you can control someone to whoever the {{A|Werewolf}} is killing which results in the witched target dying to rampage along with the {{A|Werewolf's}} target. However, beware of {{A|Bodyguards}} since you might cause a {{A|Werewolf}} to die.
*{{A|Vampires}} are another possible ally. But be warned that when {{A|Vampires}} try to bite you, you will either die or lose your {{A|Basic Defense}} instead of being converted, despite the fact that you can still win with them. If you find a {{A|Vampire}}, [[Chat (ToS)#Whispering|whisper]] to them as soon as possible so they can know not to bite you. Their numbers will often help prevent you from being [[hanged]], but at {{A|night}} you still need to be careful of the {{A|Mafia}} and {{A|Neutral Killings}}. If they suspect you of siding with the {{A|Vampires}}, they could view you as a threat and kill you.
*The {{A|Witch}} is unique among other evil roles in that unlike the {{A|Serial Killer}}, you generally need to cooperate with someone else to win, as you lack a reliable way to communicate unlike the {{A|Mafia}}.
*If you've decided on one evil role you want to side with, it can often be advantageous to throw other evils at a confirmed {{A|Veteran}}. Either they'll kill the {{A|Veteran}} for you, or they'll die and won't be a problem anymore. This can be a particularly useful way to rid yourself of a {{A|Werewolf}} or {{A|Arsonist}}, who can be dangerous to side with (and beneficial for you to kill) for the reasons outlined above.
*''Publicly'' claiming {{A|Witch}} is dangerous since it exposes you to {{A|Vigilantes}}, the {{A|Jailor}}, the un-revealed {{A|Mayor}}, and any evil role who fears you'll side with someone else, not to mention the mundane danger of being [[hanged]]. However, it's also often necessary when you have failed to identify your allies; waiting too long to claim is equally dangerous, since evil roles will very likely kill you as they wrap up the game.

===Claiming a role===
*If you are going to claim a {{A|Town Investigative}} role, an easy one to choose is {{A|Lookout}}. Not only is a {{A|Lookout}}’s {{A|Last Will}} easy to make up (much better than an {{A|Investigator}}’s), and you can also use who you controlled to write down visits. If you control player A to visit player B, write in your fake {{A|Lookout}} {{A|Last Will}} that you watched player B and that they were visited by A. Any accurate info besides writing "no visits" in a fake {{A|Lookout}} {{A|Last Will}} will often be the difference between you being [[hanged]] and being pardoned by the {{A|Town}}. As the {{A|Lookout}} fits within your investigative results, in the case one investigates you, this is a good possible claim.
**Keep in mind though that in games with more experienced players, you might be doubted due to never "watching" the {{A|Jailor}} or only seeing visiting players that were controlled. If you believe the {{A|Jailor}} was {{A|Cleaned}} without revealing, however, you may try to claim {{A|Lookout}}.
*Acting like a {{A|Jester}} or {{A|Executioner}} is another way to reduce the risk that the {{A|Mafia}} will target you, since they don't need to kill those roles; however, you face the risk of being killed by a {{A|Vigilante}} or {{A|Jailor}}, or hung by a {{A|Town}} that misreads your signal. Additionally, this clashes with your {{A|Investigator's}} results; since a neither a {{A|Lookout}} nor {{A|Forger}} would generally pretend to be either, {{A|Investigators}} are likely to assume you are evil.
*A good claim is {{A|Investigator}}, since you know the role of the target you claimed, but remember to write an {{A|Investigator's}} type of {{A|Last Will}}. Your ability gives you a degree of information, which (in an emergency) you can use to try and confirm yourself as an investigative by outing a {{A|night}} killer you previously controlled. This can be particularly useful for discarding one evil role when you've decided to side with another (e.g. to rid of the {{A|Werewolf}} or {{A|Arsonist}} when you intend to side with the {{A|Mafia}}, {{A|Vampires}} or a {{A|Serial Killer}}). This should be done with caution, however, for a variety of reasons. You ultimately need to side with one evil role or another to win; and what's worse, is that convincing the evil roles that you're a {{A|Town Investigative}} role might have you killed by the players who are supposed to be on your side. Additionally, the target you controlled will know they were controlled and might figure out that that's how you identified them, outing you with their last words or in their {{A|Last Will}}. If you are making an {{A|Investigator's}} {{A|Last Will}}, make sure that you put the right results and the roles in the right order. Otherwise, you could be seen as a struggling {{A|Consigliere}}. Refer to the {{A|Investigator}} page for complete results.
**A way to go around this is to scramble your {{A|night}} visits of whom you controlled. In other words, move your target from one {{A|night}} to a different {{A|night}}, and so on. Of course, a smart player could crack the code, and you could be busted by a {{A|Lookout}}.
*You should almost always declare that you are actually a {{A|Witch}} in the moment you think the {{A|Town}} no longer has a majority. This can help protect you from being [[hanged]] or killed, especially if there's an {{A|Arsonist}}. Even when the {{A|Town}} still has killing roles or still has a majority, it can often be safer than you might think to declare your true role; first, many players will take you for a {{A|Jester}}, and second, you're low-priority from the {{A|Town's}} perspective - you can't kill directly, and they don't actually have to kill you to win (even if there is a {{A|Vigilante}}: if the {{A|Vigilante}} dies the {{A|Town}} wins without harming you). This doesn't mean it's ''safe''; {{A|Vigilantes}} and the {{A|Jailor}} in particular are a huge danger to an exposed {{A|Witch}}. But you have to weigh this against the protection you receive from the {{A|Mafia}} and from {{A|Neutral Killing}} roles.
*Before the 2.2.0.8155 update, the {{A|Survivor}} was the most used {{A|Witch}} claim because the invest results were the same. However, you can still claim it if you don't have a better claim because it also can explain why you have {{A|Basic Defense}}.
*Like {{A|Investigator}}, a viable claim is {{A|Sheriff}}. Using what you find from other players's investigations, you can help side with the team you want to win with as well as making the {{A|Town}} trust you. However, {{A|Sheriff}} {{A|Last Wills}} are easily made, so even if you claim you found a {{A|Neutral Killing}} role and are siding with the {{A|Mafia}}, the {{A|Town}} may suspect you as an {{A|Executioner}} and [[hang]] you. This is because {{A|Sheriff}} is a common {{A|Executioner}} claim. Another advantage to the {{A|Sheriff}} claim is that, if you should discover a non-{{A|Godfather}} member of the {{A|Mafia}}, you can signal to them that you are on their side by publicly stating that they are not suspicious. Be cautious, of course, since this will put you under suspicion if they are caught later!
*Another good fake claim is {{A|Tavern Keeper}}. After finding another evil role that you want to team up with, you can tell the player that you [[Role block|role blocked]] them, and in general, this information will give them enough info to know your role while still making the {{A|Town}} trust you. Similarly, you can claim {{A|Transporter}} and tell your ally that you transported them.

===General notes===
*When using your power to try and find out what players are, remember that your primary goal is to impede the {{A|Town}} and to support evil roles; above all else, you want to avoid ''harming'' the evil roles you need to win with. Therefore, try to make sure your second target is {{A|Town}}, or at least non-evil, and try to avoid deliberately controlling evil roles unless you're certain you can benefit from it or if you're worried they'll attack you if you don't. If you've identified someone as evil, don't control players into them unless you know what you're doing, since you probably don't want to kill them by accident.
*When you're experimenting, '''never make your victim self-target''' (unless for achievement purpose or if you're absolutely certain you know what you're doing). There are several ways it can go wrong:
**If they're a {{A|Mafioso}}, you'll kill an ally if there's no {{A|Godfather}}.
**If they're an {{A|Ambusher}}, they'll kill you if you do not have your {{A|Basic Defense}}.
**If they're a {{A|Werewolf}} on a {{A|Full Moon}} {{A|night}}, you will force them to stay home, killing you in the process.
**Most {{A|Townies}} won't have any special effect when self-targeting; you'll waste their turn, but you generally do that by targeting them anywhere else too, and you'll receive info depending on the role.
**The only {{A|Townie}} who will really benefit from you forcing to self-target is the {{A|Vigilante}}. You can often force the {{A|Vigilante}} to shoot another {{A|Townie}}, in which case the {{A|Vigilante}} will commit suicide the following {{A|night}}.
*Roles like the {{A|Investigator}}, {{A|Lookout}}, {{A|Doctor}}, {{A|Bodyguard}}, and {{A|Sheriff}} will not know who they are visiting if you control them into a certain player. For example, if a {{A|Sheriff}} chooses to interrogate the {{A|Mafioso}}, but is controlled by a {{A|Witch}} to look at the {{A|Doctor}}, the result would be the same as if they had originally chosen to interrogate the {{A|Doctor}}. For {{A|Town Investigatives}}, however, because the user knows they are controlled, they will usually end up scrapping the data for that {{A|night}}, regardless of the outcome, since there is typically no way of finding out who they ended up actually investigating.
*In the case of you controlling a {{A|Consigliere}} into another player, the first message you will receive is based on your control results (which should be the {{A|Consigliere}} you controlled). The second message you will receive is the {{A|Consigliere's}} results on your second target. Keep this in mind to avoid confusion on which target is which.

==Advanced Notes==
===Friendships===
*If you found the {{A|Godfather}} or {{A|Serial Killer}} and want to make sure they do not attack you if you stop controlling them, you should [[whisper]] to them not to attack you.
*Whispering to a killing role that you have found is incredibly beneficial and most of the time they will accept your help. After all, you both have basically the same goals in mind and killing roles are usually alone, with the exception of {{A|Godfather}} unless all of the {{A|Mafia}} have died, thus partnering with you ensures them your support and another vote, which can be devastating.
*However, in the unlikely event that the killing role that you have found decides to turn against you, you may want to "inform" them that their names will be in your {{A|Last Will}}, along with their roles and also the reason why you believed they were bad. This will most probably either enrage them and make them kill you out of spite, in which case you may as well screw them over, or make them cooperate. This is a bit of a dodgy, unwanted friendship, but sometimes still needed for you to win.
*While you can't directly use them to win, you can impede the {{A|Town}} by assisting {{A|Executioners}} and {{A|Jesters}} you discovered through your ability. In [[All Any]], you can even side with an {{A|Executioner}} by backing them up when they make an accusation (claiming another {{A|Town Investigative}} role if necessary), then claiming to be a second {{A|Executioner}} yourself to protect yourself from being killed at {{A|night}}. It's not unusual to have multiple {{A|Executioners}} with the same target in that mode.

===Who to control and when===
You want to ensure as many {{A|Town}} members die as possible and that your killing friend is not suspected. As a {{A|Witch}}, more attention to the roles is needed as you need to ensure that your allies survive. {{A|Townies}} are always the first killing priority because if worse comes to worst, they are the only group you cannot win with.

For example: You have found the {{A|Mafioso}} early in the game and you know who the rest of the {{A|Mafia}} are. If one of the {{A|Mafia}} (e.g. the {{A|Blackmailer}} or {{A|Consigliere}}) are found out by an {{A|Investigator}}, then you may want to attempt to destroy their plan or ensure that the {{A|Town}} forgets about it. You can do this by telling the {{A|Mafia}} to attack someone who was supporting the accusation and you yourself can control a {{A|Doctor}} to heal the accused. This weakens the accusation and lessens the chance the accused player will be shot by a {{A|Vigilante}} or executed by a {{A|Jailor}}.

The reason why you should not be attacking an {{A|Investigator}} or {{A|Sheriff}} themselves is that this generally enrages the {{A|Town}} more and most players will make the connection that whom they have accused has killed them to try and rid of any suspicion. The {{A|Investigator}} or {{A|Sheriff}} may become a matter and in most likelihood, the accused will be [[hanged]].

===Dangers===
The {{A|Witch}} can be incredibly powerful and incredibly damaging; however, it is up to you to use your mind and figure out what to do. But be aware of potential dangers.

{{A|Lookout}}: If you have been consistently controlling a {{A|Town Protective}} role to heal yourself then a smart {{A|Lookout}} might visit the {{A|Town Protective}} to see who has been visiting them. Ignore this danger if the {{A|Town Protective}} role has not been saying they are repeatedly controlled or if you desperately need protection that {{A|night}}. Also, if you control a {{A|Lookout}} into themselves, another {{A|Lookout}} could notice this and call you out.

{{A|Veteran}}: Pay attention to who this role has killed and guess/estimate how many {{A|Alerts}} they have left. If they have used all 3 then do not bother killing them, as they cannot do anything anymore, and are not worth your time. However, if they have confirmed their role as {{A|Veteran}}, and they do not have any alerts left, then, it will be best to kill them off to lower the town’s majority voting power.

{{A|Investigators}}: An {{A|Investigator}} will see you as {{A|Lookout}}, {{A|Forger}} or {{A|Witch}}; since this has one {{A|Town}} option, many of them will likely call you out. {{A|Witches}} are low-priority from the {{A|Town's}} perspective (since you don't kill directly), but you could still be killed, especially if no other leads have been found. {{A|Lookout}} is not the worst claim, but since it's only one {{A|Town}} role as opposed to two evil roles, you'll likely be pressed harder. Sometimes, the appropriate thing to do is admit to being a {{A|Witch}}; this may allow evils to side with you, and late in the game (when they're at risk of losing the majority if they don't hang a killer), the {{A|Town}} might not be able to spare the time to hang you. It is best not to admit your identity in {{A|Ranked}}. If it's early in the game, you can accuse the {{A|Investigator}} of being an {{A|Executioner}}, but this is dangerous, since they can often prove themselves by posting their will. 

Others: {{A|Sheriffs}} are not a huge danger to you, since you show up as not suspicious; if you ''are'' accused by one, either you were framed, or it's a {{A|Jester}} or other evil roles trying to stir up suspicion. React the way an innocent {{A|Townie}} would; ask for their {{A|Last Will}} or other details to prove their role, suggest you may have been framed (which a {{A|Spy}} may be able to confirm), call them out as an {{A|Executioner}} when they fail to provide corroborating information and so on.

Early game: You are most vulnerable early game because you do not know any roles and you will be discovered immediately if someone simply points out that there is a {{A|Witch}}. In the early part of the game, pay attention to how players act and what that may mean their role is. Discovering an important role early is your precious key to winning the game, so use whatever you can to protect it (without having yourself [[hanged]], obviously). However, if there seems to be solid evidence that your potential ally has been discovered, it would be smarter to add to the prosecution and completely ignore them. Early game is when most {{A|Witches}} will fall short. If someone suspects you of being a {{A|Witch}}, it’s best you don't control them, because then they may call you out to strengthen their claim, and you will immediately be [[hanged]] or killed by a {{A|Vigilante}} or {{A|Jailor}}.

{{A|Last Will}}: In your {{A|Last Will}}, you can keep a record of who you control and what. This is useful for your own records, and the knowledge that your {{A|Last Will}} likely incriminates them will keep smarter {{A|night}} killers from killing you if they've identified you. If you wish, you can delete or edit your {{A|Last Will}} if you expect to be hung in order to avoid incriminating evil roles; however, note that you're not required to do so. {{A|Witches}} have to be alive to win and always lose the game when killed, so (unlike actual members of the {{A|Mafia}} and {{A|Vampires}}) it's not [[gamethrowing]] for you to incriminate your fellow evil roles when you die. Just remember that evil roles that are involved in two or more consecutive games may kill the {{A|Witch}} out of spite before the {{A|Witch}} can find them.

Another danger you have to be aware of is your {{A|Basic Defense}} can be used against you by attacking roles as you do not provide feedback to the attacker that removes your protection. A smart {{A|Vigilante}} will immediately reveal to {{A|Town}} that you are a {{A|Witch}} while evils can discreetly out you in order to waste the {{A|Town's}} hang for the day. This can also be an advantage as evils will know you are a potential ally to them when it is revealed you survived their attack.

