[[File:Classicgamemodes.png|alt=The Classic "Normal" Game Modes.|thumb|The Classic "Normal" Game Modes.]]In the base content of [[Town of Salem]], there are nine different game modes to play: {{A|Classic Mode}}, {{A|Ranked Practice}}, {{A|Ranked}}, [[#Custom Mode|Custom Mode]], {{A|Rapid Mode}}, {{A|All Any}}, {{A|Rainbow Mode}}, {{A|Dracula's Palace}} and {{A|Town Traitor}}.

In every game mode except [[#Custom Mode|Custom Mode]] and {{A|Rapid Mode}}, the game will start automatically when the lobby reaches 15 players.

There are also [[Game_Modes_-_Coven|Coven equivalents to all modes]] (except {{A|Rainbow Mode}}, {{A|Dracula's Palace}}, and {{A|Rapid Mode}}), plus three {{A|Coven Exclusive}} game modes ({{A|Mafia Returns}}, {{A|Lovers Mode}}, and {{A|VIP}}).

=={{Arch|Classic Mode}}==
Classic Mode is the simplest game mode as almost all of the roles are predetermined and somewhat balanced. This mode is geared towards newer players so they can learn roles without them changing every other game; the only alternating slots are {{A|Random Town}} and {{A|Town Killing}}. This game mode will probably be the very first game a player will ever play, however, this means that if experienced players do decide to play classic, there will be little claim space if they get an {{A|Evil}} role, but they can trick the new players into thinking they're {{A|Townies}}.

The {{A|Town}} should press everyone for claims. If two people claim the same role, one is either the {{A|Random Town}} or an evil role. If 2 groups of two people claim the same role (for example, two people claim {{A|Sheriff}}, and two other people claim {{A|Doctor}}) one of them is definitely evil. The same goes if 3 people claim the same role, or if 2 people claim the same role when the {{A|Random Town}} slot is dead or taken. Once the {{A|Random Town}} is known, it is easy to narrow down who is evil by process of elimination. If you are evil, you should claim a role that fits in your investigation result that is hard to disprove and provide a will to back your claim up. If the {{A|Investigator}} dies and didn't put your investigation result in their {{A|Last Will}}, you can claim any role; though it's possible the {{A|Random Town}} is another {{A|Investigator}}. Be careful of counterclaims though, normally, the one with a better {{A|Last Will}} is the one who stays alive. If you are a {{A|Jester}}, you should try and claim what a confirmed {{A|Town}} claims. Most people will likely believe them over you. If you are an {{A|Executioner}}, claim {{A|Sheriff}} or {{A|Investigator}} and accuse your target late game or counter claim your target's claim.

Since members {{A|Town}} may be new to the game, and don't usually have any clue what to do, they might ignore people's evidence and info, and randomly vote/accuse players of wrongdoing. This can be somewhat useful to the {{A|Town}} as stated above.

