==='''Strategy'''===

* The {{A|VIP}} themselves has to factor in the pros and cons of how they use their role:
** {{A|Psychic}}, {{A|Medium}}, and {{A|Retributionist}} are simple -- they can't visit; thus, they are the hardest for {{A|Coven}} to deduce.
** {{A|Mayor}} is also difficult to deduce, but due to {{A|Doctor}} being unable to visit them after they reveal, the {{A|Mayor}} needs to be careful about revealing. Therefore, you should only reveal if the {{A|Town}} is at risk of losing majority, as you are already a confirmed {{A|Townie}} to the {{A|Town}}.
** {{A|Transporter}} is an interesting case -- they can keep the {{A|Crusader}} safe, and the {{A|Crusader}} can protect them from anything. However, they can unintentionally swap themselves with a {{A|Medusa}} or the target of another {{A|Crusader}}, ending up dead.
** {{A|Tavern Keeper's}} greatest threat is the {{A|Coven Leader}}, who can send their {{A|Crusader}} anywhere. Once the {{A|Coven Leader}} is dead, it is generally safe for the {{A|VIP}} {{A|Tavern Keeper}} to target risky individuals.
** {{A|Sheriff}} and {{A|Tracker}} have the greatest catch 22 -- produce no results, and it's obvious you're the {{A|VIP}}. Go out, and you may walk into a bewitched {{A|Crusader}} or {{A|Medusa}}. If you must go out with the {{A|Coven Leader}} still alive, try to visit suspicious people who aren't attracting much attention.
** A {{A|Doctor}} {{A|VIP}} will either heal a {{A|Crusader}} {{A|Night 1}} or walk into a {{A|Medusa}}. Claiming D1 is probably not a good idea, as {{A|Coven}} may use the "peel the layers" strategy and kill you {{A|Night 2}}.
** A {{A|Crusader}} {{A|VIP}} is the most likely to fail, as the odds of another {{A|Crusader}} claim being {{A|Medusa}} is high, their status as such is obvious if no one claims, and yet claiming at all may result from a quick kill by the {{A|Potion Master}}. To further exacerbate this, traps tend to often fail on a {{A|Crusader}} -- particularly against the {{A|Coven Leader}}.
** If you're in a good position, consider voting innocent on a known {{A|Coven}} member. This could bait the {{A|Coven}} to accuse you, and subsequently out themselves. The downside is that this can also bait a {{A|Pirate}} who claims to be working for {{A|Town}}.
* Town Strategies revolve around keeping the {{A|VIP}} alive, but also finding the right balance between openness and subtlety:
** The {{A|VIP}} is '''always''' a {{A|Town}} user, and cannot be a {{A|Vigilante}} or {{A|Bodyguard}}.
** '''Do not name the {{A|VIP}} in your {{A|Last Will}}.''' Replace their name with 'VIP' or a similar contraction. Writing the {{A|VIP}}'s name in your will is considered to be [[Town of Salem Rules#Reportable Offenses|gamethrowing]].
*** There is an exception if you're {{A|Psychic}}. Instead of writing down 'VIP' in your visions, write their actual name. Writing 'VIP' will make it easier for {{A|Coven}} to deduce the {{A|VIP}}, considering their name never shows up.
** If both {{A|Potion Master}} and the {{A|Random Coven}} are dead and a user is on trial and they claim a {{A|Town}} role, ask them who the {{A|VIP}} is. If they answer incorrectly (or don't answer whatsoever), they are most likely evil. Do not do this otherwise, as a clever {{A|Coven}} member could swiftly assassinate the {{A|VIP}}, as depicted below.
** {{A|Psychic}}, {{A|Trapper}}, and {{A|Sheriff}} are all crucial roles. {{A|Psychic}} is a top priority for the {{A|Coven}} as they somewhat reliably find the evil roles, while overall persuading the {{A|Town}} to talk and figure out who their allies are.
** If the {{A|Potion Master}} kills on the first {{A|night}}, it may be wise for a {{A|Trapper}} to not lay their {{A|trap}} until the third {{A|night}}. This will ensure that your {{A|trap}} isn't wasted on a {{A|night}} the {{A|Coven Leader}} can't be killed by it, as well as give you more time to find at-risk targets, or to protect an exposed {{A|VIP}}.
** Beware of making common mistakes that help pinpoint the {{A|VIP}}. Explained in the {{A|Coven}} section.
** If at least 3 players claim {{A|Town Protective}} on the first {{A|day}}, one is liable to be a {{A|Medusa}}.
** Multiple people claiming to be {{A|Town Protective}} on the first {{A|day}} can also give {{A|Coven}} a blueprint for wiping out the {{A|VIP}}'s protection in 1-2 {{A|nights}}. If someone has claimed {{A|Crusader}} before you, it may be wise to refrain from immediately claiming yourself.
** Not claiming {{A|Crusader}} can make it difficult for the {{A|Coven Leader}} to mess with you, but if two {{A|Crusaders}} are silent on the first {{A|day}}, they will kill each other, dealing a huge blow to {{A|Town}}. Try to coordinate who is on the {{A|VIP}}, e.g. if you are a {{A|Crusader}} and somebody else claims to be {{A|Crusader}} D1 and will be on the {{A|VIP}}, do not go on the {{A|VIP}} in case you kill each other. Instead, be on them.
** Exposing the {{A|Vigilante}} can deal a devastating blow to {{A|Town}}, as a controlled {{A|Vigilante}} can easily result in the net loss of two {{A|Town}} members. You can mitigate this risk somewhat by favoring the {{A|Tracker}} or {{A|Sheriff's}} results to the {{A|Psychic's}}.
*** If a {{A|Vigilante}} is exposed on the second {{A|day}}, and you're a second {{A|Crusader}}, consider protecting the {{A|Vigilante}} that {{A|night}}. They can be forced to shoot, but you are likely to kill the {{A|Coven Leader}}, which is more than a fair trade, provided the {{A|Potion Master}} did not heal them that {{A|Night}}.
* {{A|Coven}} strategies are far more complex - to have any chance at winning, {{A|Coven}} needs to know what they're doing, while also being on the lookout for means to find and kill the {{A|VIP}}:
** A key strategy to remember, if the {{A|VIP}} is discovered early on, the {{A|Coven}} can safely attempt a quick kill by controlling the {{A|Crusader}} onto the {{A|Coven Leader}}, and having a {{A|Potion Master}} or {{A|Poisoner}} attack the {{A|VIP}}. The {{A|Coven Leader}} will be protected from any second {{A|Crusader}} or {{A|Bodyguard}} by the bewitched {{A|Crusader}}, and the controlled {{A|Crusader}} can also kill any {{A|Sheriffs}} or {{A|Trackers}} visiting the {{A|Coven Leader}}.
** The {{A|Coven Leader}} can send random undeclared {{A|Town}} members to an alert {{A|Medusa}} to open up claim space, but there is a chance they may target a {{A|Pirate}}, non-visiting role, or the {{A|VIP}}. The {{A|Potion Master}} can heal them to help manage risk.
** {{A|Coven's}} {{A|VIP}} is the {{A|Coven Leader}}. Their arsenal of tricks is so vast that to lose the {{A|Coven Leader}} early is to all but forfeit the match:
*** A {{A|Coven Leader}}, starting on {{A|Night 3}}, cannot be harmed by a {{A|Crusader}}.
*** Sometimes, a {{A|Coven Leader}} can break up a double {{A|Crusader}} combo, which always includes the {{A|VIP}}, by sending one of the {{A|Crusaders}} to themselves. There is a chance that the controlled {{A|Crusader}} will attack their partner.
*** If a {{A|Bodyguard}} is protecting a {{A|Crusader}} starting from {{A|Night 3}}, this can be circumvented by the {{A|Coven Leader}} sending the {{A|Crusader}} to the {{A|Coven Leader}}. The {{A|Bodyguard}} will die protecting the {{A|Crusader}}, but the {{A|Crusader}} will protect the {{A|Coven Leader}}.
*** If you know the {{A|VIP}}, and know the order in which two {{A|Crusaders}} are protecting them, send the exposed {{A|Crusader}} to the {{A|VIP}} -- both {{A|Crusaders}} will kill each other, leaving the {{A|VIP}} exposed the next {{A|night}}.
** The {{A|Medusa}} is also a key player, as it can wipe out a number of players early on, sometimes score {{A|Night 1}} kills, and overall is best as a suicide lead:
*** Claiming to be a {{A|Crusader}} on the {{A|VIP}} as {{A|Medusa}} can leave the {{A|VIP}} exposed and cause actual {{A|Town Protectives}} to be killed. You'll likely be found out within a couple of {{A|nights}}, but you can do a lot of damage. Since {{A|Medusa}} isn't part of the quick kill strategy, it's often worth the risk.
*** Alternatively, claiming to be "on the {{A|Crusader}}", and having the {{A|Coven Leader}} send the {{A|Crusader}} to you, is a decent way to lure other {{A|Town Protectives}}, potentially including the {{A|Tracker}} and/or {{A|Sheriff}} towards you. You'll score a lot of kills, but you'll likely be hanged the next {{A|day}}.
*** Given that the {{A|Medusa}} can only make risky contact kills with the {{A|Necronomicon}}, it is the second-weakest end-game role to {{A|Poisoner}}. It is best used early and then expended to protect the other {{A|Coven}} members from being hanged.
** The {{A|Potion Master}} is critical to quick kill strategies, but also has a few other tricks:
*** Keeping the {{A|Coven Leader}} alive is your top priority. If they're doing something risky, like messing with {{A|Town Protectives}} on the first {{A|night}}, consider healing them. If the {{A|Coven Leader}} sends a {{A|Town Protective}} to themselves, though, do not heal them -- you may be killed by a {{A|Crusader}}.
*** Consider holding your attack for a {{A|night}} or two, and try figuring out who the {{A|VIP}} is. This may also cast suspicion on those who are role blocked.
*** On the second {{A|day}}, consider using your investigation potion on a high-priority target, regardless of whether or not you know their role. This will dismantle any trap that has been put up.
** The general play-style varies based on which {{A|Random Coven}} role you end up with:
*** The {{A|Hex Master}} prioritizes wiping out {{A|Doctors}} and {{A|Crusaders}} ASAP. Even if you lose your {{A|Coven Leader}}, the {{A|Hex Master}} can essentially make risk-free guesses as to who the {{A|VIP}} is if only {{A|Bodyguards}} and {{A|Trappers}} remain. Without the {{A|Necronomicon}}, your job is to dismantle {{A|traps}}. Don't plan on being able to use your finishing spell - given the lack of claimspace in and the goal of VIP}}, this is extremely rare.
*** The {{A|Necromancer's}} two most useful ghouls are the {{A|Vigilante}} and {{A|Crusader}}. Unfortunately, the {{A|Necromancer}} has poor synergy with the {{A|Medusa}}, and requires strategies that involve straight up killing as many {{A|Town Protective}} roles as possible. With the {{A|Necronomicon}}, you have a '''basic''' attack, but this can still be prevented by traps and {{A|Bodyguards}}.
*** The {{A|Poisoner}} strategies revolve around having the {{A|Coven Leader}} harass the lead {{A|Crusader}}, and the safest move is to send said {{A|Crusader}} to the {{A|Coven Leader}}, while the {{A|Potion Master}} and {{A|Poisoner}} liberally seek out target {{A|VIP}}. The {{A|Poisoner}} is the worst late-game role, so they are most useful in attempting a quick kill and are expendable if it means protecting the {{A|Coven Leader}} from being hanged.
** Finally, be on the lookout for tells that help narrow down who is and isn't the {{A|VIP}}:
*** Pay attention to any {{A|Pirate}} or {{A|Guardian Angel}} claims, putting them on your "not {{A|VIP}}" list.
*** If a {{A|Psychic}} excludes the {{A|VIP}} from results, keep a list of everyone who IS named. Include fellow living {{A|Coven}} if it's in your will, so as to misdirect the {{A|Town}}.
*** Also, pay attention for anyone explicitly not grilled when named by the {{A|Psychic}} - they may be the {{A|VIP}}, but they may also be someone who claimed before.
*** If a {{A|Sheriff}} or {{A|Tracker}} has no results for {{A|days}}, or a {{A|Town Protective}} claim is oddly absent or accomplishing nothing, they may be the {{A|VIP}}, staying home.
*** Similarly, you may wish to add active {{A|Sheriffs}} and {{A|Trackers}} to your "not {{A|VIP}}" list, as their {{A|VIP}} counterparts often stay home.
* Neutrals are wildcards, but they have their own considerations:
** {{A|Guardian Angel}} may wish to refrain from declaring or voting until they know what side they're on. Declaring D1 will prevent you from being able to lie to save your target, and may make you the target of {{A|Pirates}} who don't wish to antagonize {{A|Town}}.
** A {{A|Guardian Angel}} may wish to write up a couple of fake wills for their target, but keep it in a separate text file so as not to reveal them upon death. If the {{A|Psychic}}, {{A|Tracker}}, or {{A|Sheriff}} implicates them, try to defend your target with a false {{A|Tracker}} or {{A|Sheriff}} will that makes them look innocent. In the event of a {{A|Sheriff}}, you may need to simply counterclaim {{A|Sheriff}}. You merely need to keep them from being hanged that {{A|day}}.
** {{A|Pirates}} can be cowed to help the {{A|Town}}, but it is overall risky to keep them around. If {{A|Coven}} is near voting majority, it's generally best to let them live.

=={{Arch|Lovers Mode}}==
"This mode is a Town vs Killers mode that will randomly pair people as Lovers. Keep your lover alive, regardless of their faction!"

Coven Lovers is a game mode that rotates weekly and the counterpart of [[# VIP|VIP]] and {{A|Town Traitor (Coven)}}. It has a normal role list, yet 8/15 players are tied to another as lovers (this means the 7 other players are left single). There are a number of special rules governing lovers:

* If one member of a pair of lovers dies, the other is dealt the equivalent of an Unstoppable Attack. {{A|Pestilence's}} absolute immunity to being killed at {{A|night}} will protect him from this.
** If the {{A|Plaguebearer}} has become {{A|Pestilence}} and they have a lover, then when their lover dies, they won't die. Instead, they will receive the message "You care not for a lover, for a horseman feels no love, no pain, no happiness".[[File:SpecialLabel_Lovers.png|alt=|thumb|Icon that appears next to your lovers name on the player list.]][[File:RoleIcon_LoversWins.png|thumb|100x100px|Lovers Icon]]
* If one player suicides (leaves the game), their lover will not die.
* Lovers are able to secretly [[Chat (ToS)#Whispering|whisper]] to each other without the whisper notification showing up in the chat box to others.
* Lovers can win with each other's factions. For example, if the {{A|Plaguebearer}} has a living {{A|Town}} lover, then they will be able to win with the {{A|Town}}, and will not block a {{A|Town}} victory.
* If all players who do not have lovers die, all lovers still alive will win even if they are in opposition (for example, if the only living players are a pair of {{A|Town}} lovers and a pair consisting of {{A|Serial Killer}} and {{A|Werewolf}}, all four will win).
* {{A|Bodyguards}} cannot be (nor have) lovers under any circumstances because the {{A|Bodyguard}} will die in the process of saving another target, thereby killing their lover.
* Aside from that, any pair of roles can be lovers.

